'''Larry Robert Rhoden''' (born February 5, 1959)<ref>{{cite web|title=Former State Rep. Larry Rhoden|url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/194549/Larry_Robert_Rhoden.html|publisher=[[LegiStorm]]}}</ref> is an American politician and businessman serving since 2025 as the 34th [[governor of South Dakota]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-25 |title=Larry Rhoden succeeds to governor of South Dakota |url=https://www.keloland.com/news/local-news/larry-rhoden-to-become-governor-of-south-dakota/ |access-date=2025-01-25 |website=KELOLAND.com |language=en-US}}</ref> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], Rhoden served from 2019 to 2025 as the 39th [[Lieutenant Governor of South Dakota]] and from 2009 to 2015 and again from 2017 until 2019 in the [[South Dakota Legislature]].

A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], Rhoden served from 2001 to 2009 as a member of the [[South Dakota House of Representatives]], from 2009 to 2015 and from 2017 to 2019 as a member of the [[South Dakota Senate]], and from 2019 to 2025 as the 39th [[lieutenant governor of South Dakota]].<ref name="gov_off" /><ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=503989 |title= Our Campaigns – SD State Senate 29 Race – Nov 04, 2008}}</ref><ref name= "ballotpedia.org">{{cite web |url= https://ballotpedia.org/Larry_Rhoden |title= Larry Rhoden}}</ref> He ran for [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] in the [[2014 United States Senate election in South Dakota|2014]] election, losing in the Republican primary to former governor [[Mike Rounds]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Weigel |first= David |date= 2013-07-31 |title= Mike Rounds, the Republican Most Likely to Take Over a Democratic Senate Seat, and His Struggles With the Tea Party |url= https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2013/07/mike-rounds-the-republican-most-likely-to-take-over-a-democratic-senate-seat-and-his-struggles-with-the-tea-party.html |access-date= 2023-01-06 |website= Slate Magazine |language=en}}</ref> In January 2025, he became governor after Governor [[Kristi Noem]] resigned to become [[United States Secretary of Homeland Security]].<ref name= ":0" />

==Early life and education==
Rhoden was born and raised on a farm. He grew up attending church, and graduated from Sunshine Bible Academy in 1977. After high school, he served in the [[South Dakota National Guard]] from 1978 to 1985. While his children were young, he served as a church trustee and on the local school board, and led the board of directors for the area [[CHS Inc.|Cenex]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://kristiforgovernor.com/meet-larry/|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180825030415/http://kristiforgovernor.com/meet-larry/|archive-date = August 25, 2018|title = Governor Kristi Noem – South Dakota: America's Beacon of Freedom}}</ref>

==Early political career==

=== South Dakota House and Senate ===
Rhoden served in the [[South Dakota House of Representatives]] from 2001 to 2008, including four years as majority leader. After being term-limited, he was elected to the state senate. In 2010, Rhoden ran for senate majority leader, but lost to Russell Olson.<ref name=Smokeout>{{cite news|last=Montgomery|first=David|title=Who is Larry Rhoden?|url=http://politicalsmokeout.tumblr.com/post/55002209199/who-is-larry-rhoden|access-date=August 16, 2013|newspaper=Argus Leader|date=July 9, 2013}}</ref>

Rhoden served on the Agriculture and Natural Resources and the State Affairs Committees.<ref>{{cite web|title=Senator Larry Rhoden|url=http://legis.state.sd.us/sessions/2013/MemberDetail.aspx?Member=88|publisher=South Dakota Legislature|access-date=August 16, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130901175502/http://legis.state.sd.us/sessions/2013/MemberDetail.aspx?Member=88|archive-date=September 1, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> He backed bills to arm volunteers in schools, and sponsored a legislative finding that the "[[Founding Fathers of the United States|Founding Fathers]] freely and willingly abjured all legislative and executive authority to regulate gun ownership and usage… to individual citizens."<ref name="Smokeout"/>

=== 2014 U.S. Senate candidacy ===
{{main|2014 United States Senate election in South Dakota}}
Rhoden ran for the U.S. Senate in 2014, calling himself a "conservative voice for limited government". He spoke out against abortion, same-sex marriage, "career politicians", "activist judges", and immigration "amnesty".<ref name=Kickoff>{{cite news|last=Montgomery|first=David|title=Rhoden strikes conservative tone in campaign kickoff|url=http://politicalsmokeout.tumblr.com/post/55089509066/rhoden-strikes-conservative-tone-in-campaign-kickoff|access-date=August 16, 2013|newspaper=Argus Leader|date=July 10, 2013}}</ref> Rhoden signed a pledge to never raise taxes, and supported de-funding the [[Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act]].<ref name="Hard line">{{cite news|last=Montgomery|first=David|title=Senate hopeful Larry Rhoden says his hard line aligns with right|url=http://www.argusleader.com/article/20130730/NEWS/307300030/Senate-hopeful-Larry-Rhoden-says-his-hard-line-aligns-right|access-date=August 16, 2013|newspaper=Argus Leader|date=July 30, 2013|archive-date=August 16, 2013|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130816155809/http://www.argusleader.com/article/20130730/NEWS/307300030/Senate-hopeful-Larry-Rhoden-says-his-hard-line-aligns-right|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Rhoden spoke at a conference organized by the conservative organization [[RedState]], criticizing fellow candidate [[Mike Rounds]]'s position on taxes.<ref name="RedState">{{cite news|last=Montgomery|first=David|title=Rhoden: Stand by your position|url=http://www.argusleader.com/article/20130803/VOICES/308030007/Rhoden-Stand-by-your-position|access-date=August 16, 2013|newspaper=Argus Leader|date=August 2, 2013|archive-date=August 16, 2013|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130816155811/http://www.argusleader.com/article/20130803/VOICES/308030007/Rhoden-Stand-by-your-position|url-status=dead}}</ref> In the June 2 primary, Rounds defeated Rhoden, 41,377 votes to 13,393.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sdsos.gov/docs/2014ElectionReturn.pdf|title=2014 Election Results|publisher=sdsos.gov|access-date=May 17, 2020}}</ref>

==Lieutenant governor of South Dakota (2019–2025)==
===2018 gubernatorial election===
{{main|2018 South Dakota gubernatorial election}}
On June 20, 2018, Republican gubernatorial nominee [[Kristi Noem]] announced that Rhoden would be her running mate. Noem had said the role of a lieutenant governor, "I would do it a little differently maybe than [[Dennis Daugaard|Daugaard]] and [[Matt Michels|Michels]] have done it... I don’t see the lieutenant governor filling as big a role as Michels did. I'm just a believer that there are certain decisions the governor has to make, and so maybe it would be more of a traditional role than what we saw in the last administration."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Woster |first1=Kevin |title=Next up for Noem: picking a running mate, planning a general-election race, holding a rummage sale |url=http://www.sdpb.org/blogs/kevinwoster/kristi-noem-on-fixing-fence-holding-a-rummage-sale-and-picking-a-lieutenant-governor-doing-the-hor/ |website=SDPB.org |date=June 11, 2018 |publisher=South Dakota Public Broadcasting}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
Rhoden took office on January 5, 2019. On May 5, 2020, Noem announced that South Dakota Secretary of Agriculture Kim Vanneman would resign effective May 8, and that Rhoden was being named interim agriculture secretary.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.sdpb.org/blogs/politics-public-policy/noem-appoints-lt-gov-as-interim-ag-secretary/ |title=Noem Appoints Lt. Gov as Interim Ag Secretary |website=SDPB.org |publisher=South Dakota Public Broadcasting|date=May 5, 2020|access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref> On August 27, Noem announced that she was merging the Department of Agriculture and the Department of Environment and Natural Resources into a Department of Agriculture and Natural Resources, to be led by Secretary Hunter Roberts, thereby ending Rhoden's role as interim secretary.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.sd.gov/newsitem.aspx?id=27215|title=Governor Noem announces Merging of Agriculture and Environment and Natural Resources Departments |publisher=news.sd.gov|date=August 27, 2020|access-date=August 30, 2020}}</ref>

On June 20, 2020, at the Republican State Convention, Rhoden was elected to be one of South Dakota's three Republican presidential electors, along with Noem and Attorney General [[Jason Ravnsborg]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://southdakotagopconvention.com/results/|title=Convention Results|publisher=southdakotagopconvention.com|date=June 20, 2020|access-date=July 7, 2020|archive-date=November 16, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201116193309/https://southdakotagopconvention.com/results/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Governor of South Dakota (2025–present)==

=== Accession ===
[[File:Meeting with Gov Larry Rhoden (54962166424).jpg|thumb|Rhoden with the [[United States Secretary of Homeland Security]] and his predecessor [[Kristi Noem]]]]
In November 2024, President-elect [[Donald Trump]] announced that he would nominate Kristi Noem for Secretary of Homeland Security. After her confirmation on January 25, 2025, she resigned as governor of South Dakota and Rhoden was sworn in by [[South Dakota Supreme Court]] Chief Justice [[Steven R. Jensen]].<ref name= ":0">{{Cite news |last1= Engelkes |first1= Cale |last2= Mercer |first2= Bob |date= 2025-01-25 |title= Larry Rhoden succeeds to governor of South Dakota |url= https://www.keloland.com/news/local-news/larry-rhoden-to-become-governor-of-south-dakota/ |access-date= 2025-01-25 |website= Keloland Local News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last= Dausch| first= Dominik| date= 2025-01-27 |title= Larry Rhoden sworn in as 34th governor of South Dakota in private ceremony| url= https://www.argusleader.com/story/news/politics/2025/01/27/larry-rhoden-sworn-in-as-34th-governor-of-south-dakota-kristi-noem/77972497007/|access-date=2025-01-31 |website= Sioux Falls Argus Leader}}</ref> On January 29, he chose [[Tony Venhuizen]] as his [[Lieutenant Governor of South Dakota|lieutenant governor]].<ref>{{Cite news| date= 2025-01-29| title= Rhoden Selects Tony Venhuizen To Serve As Lieutenant Governor |url= https://www.kccrradio.com/2025/01/29/rhoden-selects-tony-venhuizen-to-serve-as-lieutenant-governor/ |access-date= 2025-01-31| website= KCCR Radio}}</ref> Venhuizen was unanimously confirmed by the [[South Dakota Senate]] and [[South Dakota House of Representatives]] the next day.<ref>{{Cite news| date= 2025-01-30| title= HR 7001, House of Representatives, Adopt Resolution – 2025 |url= https://sdlegislature.gov/Session/Vote/80757| access-date= 2025-01-31| website= South Dakota House}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news| date= 2025-01-30| title= Senate Resolution 701| url= https://sdlegislature.gov/Session/Bill/26104 |access-date= 2025-01-31| website= South Dakota Senate}}</ref>

=== 2026 election ===
{{main|2026 South Dakota gubernatorial election}}
On April 24, 2025, Speaker of the South Dakota House of Representatives [[Jon Hansen]] announced his bid for the Republican nomination in the [[2026 South Dakota gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Huber |first= Makenzie |date= 2025-04-25 |title= First candidate for governor focuses on property rights, spending and halting 'corporate welfare' |url= https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/04/24/first-candidate-governor-race-jon-hansen-focuses-property-rights-spending-cuts-halting-corporate-welfare-karla-lems/ |access-date= 2026-07-27 |website= South Dakota Searchlight |language= en-US}}</ref>

On May 28, Aberdeen businessman Toby Doeden announced his candidacy for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Haiar |first= Joshua |date= 2025-05-29 |title= Doeden joins race for governor with pledge to eliminate property taxes |url= https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/05/28/doeden-joins-race-for-governor-with-pledge-to-eliminate-property-taxes/ |access-date= 2026-07-27 |website= South Dakota Searchlight |language= en-US}}</ref> 

On November 18, Rhoden announced his candidacy, joining Hansen, Doeden, and U.S. Representative [[Dusty Johnson]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Dausch |first= Dominik |title= Gov. Larry Rhoden to launch 2026 South Dakota governor bid at Rapid City event |url= https://www.argusleader.com/story/news/politics/2025/11/12/larry-rhoden-south-dakota-governor-campaign-announcement/87237649007/ |access-date= 2026-07-27 |website= Argus Leader |language= en-US}}</ref>

From March to June 2026, the Rhoden, Doeden, Hansen, and Johnson campaigns ran various [[Attack ad|attack ads]] against one another. In late April 2026, an attack ad specifically targeting Rhoden paid for by an out-of-state company known as Rushmore Principles began to appear on South Dakota television airwaves, which the Rhoden and Doeden campaigns accused the Johnson campaign of creating and spreading.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Santella |first= Dan |date= April 23, 2026 |title= Critical ads, dark money and SD's GOP campaign for governor |url= https://www.keloland.com/news/local-news/critical-ads-dark-money-and-sds-gop-campaign-for-governor/ |website= Keloland News}}</ref> In early May 2026, Rhoden told a group of journalists Johnson had attempted to blackmail him so that he would not enter the gubernatorial race.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Rifaat |first= Alexander |date= 2026-05-01 |title= Rhoden: Johnson tried to keep me from entering governor's race |url= https://www.dakotanewsnow.com/2026/05/01/rhoden-johnson-threatened-me-not-enter-governors-race/ |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= Dakota News Now |language= en}}</ref>

In the first round of the Republican primary on June 2, Doeden received 41,791 votes (30.6%), Rhoden 34,379 (25.2%), Johnson 31,925 (23.4%), and Hansen 28,391 (20.8%).<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-07-21 |title= South Dakota Governor Primary Election 2026 Live Results |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/south-dakota-governor-results |access-date= 2026-07-27 |website= www.nbcnews.com |language= en}}</ref> As no candidate received 35% of the primary vote, Doeden and Rhoden advanced to a runoff on July 28.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Voter Guide: 2026 Republican governor nomination runoff • South Dakota |url= https://southdakotasearchlight.com/voter-guides/2026-republican-governor-nomination-runoff/ |access-date= 2026-07-27 |website= South Dakota Searchlight |language= en-US}}</ref> The primary runoff was the first in South Dakota history since the provision was enacted ahead of the [[1986 South Dakota gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Staff |first= 270toWin |title= Live Results: Georgia Congressional Special Election, South Dakota Primary Runoff - 270toWin |url= https://www.270towin.com/news/2026/07/27/live-results-georgia-congressional-special-election-south-dakota-primary-runoff_1829.html |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= 270toWin.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-07-22 |title= 7 things to know ahead of the SD gubernatorial runoff |url= https://www.sdnewswatch.org/south-dakota-republican-gop-gubernatorial-race-larry-rhoden-toby-doeden/ |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= South Dakota News Watch |language= en}}</ref>

Rhoden won the primary runoff with 83,532 votes (70.8%) to Doeden's 34,430 (29.2%),<ref>{{Cite web| date= 2026-07-29 |title= South Dakota Governor Primary Runoff 2026 Live Results |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/south-dakota-governor-runoff-results |access-date= 2026-07-29 |website= www.nbcnews.com |language=en}}</ref> becoming the Republican nominee to run for his first full term in office.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Tupper |first= Seth |date= 2026-07-29 |title= South Dakota governor wins Republican nomination to seek his own term after succeeding Noem |url= https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2026/07/28/south-dakota-governor-wins-republican-nomination-to-seek-his-own-term-after-succeeding-noem/ |access-date= 2026-07-29 |website= South Dakota Searchlight |language= en-US}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
[[File:Larry Rhoden & Mike Huckabee.jpg|thumb|Rhoden with former [[Governor of Arkansas]] and current [[Ambassador]], [[Mike Huckabee]]]]
Early in his term, Rhoden worked with [[United States Department of the Interior|Interior Secretary]] [[Doug Burgum]] to order the return of the [[Mount Rushmore]] fireworks show in 2026.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.dakotanewsnow.com/2025/02/17/rhoden-confirms-fireworks-will-return-mt-rushmore-2026/ |title= Rhoden confirms fireworks will return to Mt. Rushmore in 2026 |date= February 17, 2025 }}</ref>

In late July 2026, after a series of intense summer storms throughout South Dakota, Rhoden declared a state of emergency in affected areas [[Beadle County, South Dakota|Beadle County]] and [[Hyde County, South Dakota|Hyde County]], and sought federal financial assistance for damages in Beadle, Hyde, [[Bennett County, South Dakota|Bennett]], [[Buffalo County, South Dakota|Buffalo]], [[Day County, South Dakota|Day]], [[Hand County, South Dakota|Hand]], [[Hughes County, South Dakota|Hughes]], and [[Stanley County, South Dakota|Stanley]] Counties.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Gov. Rhoden Declares State of Emergency |url= https://news.sd.gov/news?id=news_kb_article_view&sys_id=bc65362947718f50237fbd51026d43c5 |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= news.sd.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Gov. Rhoden Requests Presidential Disaster Declaration after Historic Storms - News |url= https://news.sd.gov/news?id=news_kb_article_view&sys_id=3bd7d1de47964b50da219464336d4365 |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= news.sd.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Huber |first= Makenzie |date= 2026-07-27 |title= Governor of South Dakota seeks federal help for damages from summer storms |url= https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2026/07/27/governor-of-south-dakota-seeks-federal-help-for-damages-from-summer-storms/ |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= South Dakota Searchlight |language= en-US}}</ref> Rhoden also requested that President Trump create a formal presidential disaster declaration regarding the damaging storms, saying: "This request would provide several million dollars in federal funding to help these communities recover. I thank President Trump in advance for his consideration and his help."<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-07-27 |title= Gov. Rhoden requests presidential disaster declaration following summer storms |url= https://www.dakotanewsnow.com/2026/07/27/gov-rhoden-requests-presidential-disaster-declaration-following-summer-storms/ |access-date= 2026-07-28 |website= Dakota News Now |language= en}}</ref>

